ide uncertainty.", "watch": "The first real New Jersey general-election polls, expert ratings, and clarity on each party's nominee — plus whether the out-party environment holds in 2028 — could move this quickly in either direction.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New Jersey enters the 2028 presidential cycle as an open-seat state: the incumbent president is term-limited, and neither party has settled on a nominee. The state's recent partisan lean favors Democrats by about four points, a notably narrower edge than New Jersey's historically larger Democratic margins. No general-election polls, expert ratings, or candidate fundraising figures are available yet, so the outlook rests on the state's lean alone.\n\nCoverage so far consists of early 2028 speculation — Chris Christie weighing a third bid, hypothetical national polls testing Democrats against JD Vance and Marco Rubio, and primary chatter in other states — none of which measures a New Jersey general election. Democrats are therefore modest favorites, with wide uncertainty until actual polling, ratings, and the eventual nominees bring the race into focus.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6761, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.642, "margin": 4.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6472, "margin": 4.0}]}